  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1053 AM MST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nUNSEASONABLY WARM AND DRY THROUGH FRIDAY:\n\nHigh pressure will persist through the work week bringing \nunseasonably warm and dry conditions. Temperatures are expected \nto run 5-10 degrees above normal through Friday.\n\nCOOLER, WETTER THIS WEEKEND:\n\nA period of unsettled weather arrives this weekend and beyond \nbeginning with a fast-moving low pressure system. Moisture-rich \nsouthwesterly advects into the Four Corners region ahead of the \nlow pressure system approaching from the west. Precipitation \nbegins Friday night as moisture advection intensifies. Mountain \nsnow is favored above 8,000-9,000 feet with valley rain below. \nIsolated thunderstorms are possible as well on Saturday \nafternoon. \n\nThere's still a lot of model spread for the QPF of this storm-  \nabout an inch between the 25th to 75th percentile over and \nadjacent to the San Juans. This means that there will likely be \nrefinements made to the rain and snow forecasts in future \nforecast packages. NBM snowfall data continues to support a high\npotential (>80% chance) for 6+ inches over the San Juans above \n9,000-10,000 feet, with a low potential (<20% chance) for 12+ \ninches. Impacts wise, slippery to hazardous travel is likely \nover mountain passes with reduced visibility while it's snowing.\n\nSnow levels drop to around 7,000 feet late Saturday night. \nHowever, precipitation chances will be winding down by then. \nLingering orographic showers could produce light snow at lower \nelevations through early Sunday morning. Temperatures through \nthe weekend will run near-normal for mid-November.\n\nLOOKING AHEAD:\n\nAn unsettled pattern remains in place next week with another \nround of valley rain and mountain snow possible by mid week. \nForecast confidence is very low at this time due to sizable \ndifferences in the timing, strength, and track of the next low \npressure system. Current trends support another fast-moving \nstorm arriving Tuesday evening.",
